dynamic host configuration protocol

the DORA process

Discover
Offer
Request
Acknowledge

Step 1: Discover (Client to Server)
The client machine powers on or connects to Wi-Fi. Because it doesn't have an IP, it sends out a DHCP Discover packet to a destination IP of 255.255.255.255 (Network Broadcast) and a destination MAC of FF:FF:FF:FF:FF:FF (Hardware Broadcast). Every machine on the local network segment receives this packet, but only a DHCP server will process it.

Analogy: Someone walking into a dark room and shouting, "Is there a landlord here?"

Step 2: Offer (Server to Client)
The DHCP server hears the broadcast. It checks its internal database—called a DHCP Scope (a pool of available IP addresses)—and reserves an available IP. It sends a DHCP Offer packet back to the network containing a proposed IP address, a subnet mask, and the lease duration.

Step 3: Request (Client to Server)
The client receives the offer. If there are multiple DHCP servers on a large corporate network, the client typically accepts the first offer it receives. It broadcasts a DHCP Request packet back to the network. It broadcasts this step to tell all the other DHCP servers: "Thank you, but I have chosen Server A's offer, so you can unlock the IPs you reserved for me."

Analogy: Shouting back, "I accept Landlord A's offer!"

Step 4: Acknowledge (Server to Client)
The chosen DHCP server receives the request. It finalizes the entry in its database, mapping the client's physical MAC address to the leased IP address. It sends a final DHCP Acknowledge (ACK) packet to the client. This packet contains the complete configuration payload: the IP address, subnet mask, default gateway, and DNS servers. The client can now communicate on the network.
